In simple words: iptables is the built‑in Linux firewall tool that controls network traffic by applying rules to decide whether packets are allowed, blocked, or modified.

-

# Block traffic from a specific IP

sudo iptables -A INPUT -s 192.168.1.100 -j DROP

# Allow HTTP traffic

sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT

- sudo → run the command with administrator privileges.

iptables → Linux firewall tool.

-A → append a new rule to the chain.

INPUT chain → handles packets coming into your system.

-s 192.168.1.100 → match packets from source IP 192.168.1.100.

-j DROP → action is to silently discard those packets.
This rule blocks all incoming traffic from the IP address

- to delete a rule
- sudo iptables -D INPUT 1
